# Requirement files suite py-prototype implements: `<requirement-id>  <snapshot the CHECK was
# authored against>`, whitespace-separated, id first.
#
# ⛔ THE SECOND COLUMN IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE CODE, NOT ABOUT THE REQUIREMENT, and it exists because
# F76 cost us a void measurement. ECP-R57's obligation INVERTED at 0.8.2.25; the requirement file and
# its item were re-authored on 2026-09-15 and `check_r57` went on scoring the .21 rule. ECP-R7's code
# was pinned at 0.8.2.24; the file said so and `check_r7` went on accepting any refusal. In BOTH
# cases `make check` was green, IMPLEMENTS still named the id, and the executed-by-a-suite ratchet
# still counted it -- because every one of those measures a DECLARATION that the suite implements the
# id, and none of them can see WHICH VERSION of the obligation the code implements.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS A DECLARATION TOO, AND IT CAN BE BUMPED WITHOUT TOUCHING THE CODE. Said out loud rather
# than pretended away: the value is not that it is unforgeable, it is that bumping it is a CONSCIOUS
# LINE IN THE DIFF where the drift was previously invisible. D16 forbids a suite asserting the
# snapshot it VALIDATES AGAINST (that must be derived -- F65); this is the different fact of which
# snapshot the code was WRITTEN against, which cannot be derived from anything and whose going stale
# is the signal we want.
#
# `unreviewed` = nobody has established which snapshot this check implements. It is the honest value
# and the ratchet in IMPLEMENTS-REVIEW-DEBT counts it down-only.
#
# Read by the Makefile (digests written into the bundle), tests/test_manifest.py (must equal the
# CHECKS table), tools/requirement-gate.py (the executed-by-a-suite ratchet), and
# tools/implements-gate.py (this column).
